Upright — NOTICE
References and provenance.

Upright is licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

It contains no third-party code. Every line was written for this library
from the physics and the measurements below; nothing is copied, ported or
translated from another implementation. The works below are cited as the
sources of the physics, of the measured numbers the law is fitted to, and
of the digital forms, nothing more.

The reference tables under extras/reference/ are transcriptions of
published numbers and figures, each file naming its source in its header;
extras/reference/README.txt names each file's source, what was transcribed
and what is unverified. They are data for the host tool and the tests; none
of them is compiled into the library.

  - B. Bank, S. Zambon and F. Fontana, "A modal-based real-time piano
    synthesizer", IEEE TASLP 18(4), 2010. The modal string, the hammer's
    discretisation and its one-sample delay, the secondary resonators, the
    soundboard as parallel second-order sections, the loss term b1 + b3 w².
    https://iris.univr.it/retrieve/e14ff6e2-e533-0209-e053-6605fe0ad24c/allegatooa_6366.pdf
  - B. Bank, "Physics-based sound synthesis of the piano", thesis, 2000, and
    "Physics-based sound synthesis of string instruments including
    geometric nonlinearities", PhD thesis, 2006. The decay times 1 / tau_k =
    b1 + b3 w_k², the multi-rate hammer. https://home.mit.bme.hu/~bank/thesis/
  - B. Bank, F. Avanzini, G. Borin, G. De Poli, F. Fontana and D.
    Rocchesso, "Physically informed signal processing methods for piano
    sound synthesis: a research overview", EURASIP JASP 2003. Chaigne and
    Askenfelt's hammer masses, stiffnesses and exponents
    (extras/reference/hammer_params.txt).
  - B. Bank and J. Chabassier, "Model-based digital pianos", IEEE SPM
    36(1), 2019. The overview.
  - A. Chaigne and A. Askenfelt, "Numerical simulations of piano strings"
    I and II, JASA 95, 1994. The hammer's power law and its parameters, the
    loss parameters (cited through Bank et al.).
  - A. Chaigne, "Reconstruction of piano hammer force from string
    velocity", JASA 140(5), 2016. Measured decay times at 1 kHz and hammer
    masses (extras/reference/decay_grand.txt).
  - J. Chabassier and M. Duruflé, "Physical parameters for piano modeling",
    INRIA RT-0425, 2012, and J. Chabassier, A. Chaigne and P. Joly,
    "Modeling and simulation of a grand piano", INRIA RR-8181, 2012. A
    Steinway-D-based model's string scale, hammers and damping per key, and
    spruce's constants (extras/reference/inharmonicity_grand_model.txt,
    hammer_scale_grand.txt, decay_grand.txt, soundboard.txt).
  - J. Jaatinen and J. Pätynen, "Effect of inharmonicity on pitch
    perception and subjective tuning of piano tones", JASA 152(2), 2022. A
    Steinway D's measured B per key (extras/reference/inharmonicity_grand.txt).
  - H. Hinrichsen, "Entropy-based tuning of musical instruments", arXiv
    1203.5101, 2012. An upright's measured B per key
    (extras/reference/inharmonicity_upright.txt).
  - A. Askenfelt and E. Jansson, "From touch to string vibrations", in
    "Five lectures on the acoustics of the piano", KTH, 1990. Hammer
    velocities and contact durations (extras/reference/hammer_params.txt);
    the key reaching its bed after the hammer meets the string (the
    attack's thud, up_attack.h).
  - F. Pfeifle, "Real-time physical model of a Wurlitzer and Rhodes
    electric piano", DAFx 2017. The tine as a beam, the Hunt & Crossley
    hammer, the magnetic pickup by Faraday's law, the reed's single mode,
    the electrostatic pickup's current.
    https://www.dafx17.eca.ed.ac.uk/papers/DAFx17_paper_79.pdf
  - M. Münster and F. Pfeifle, "Non-linear behaviour in sound production of
    the Rhodes piano", ISMA 2014. The growl is the pickup's; the tine and
    tonebar tuned apart.
  - A. Falaize and T. Hélie, "Passive simulation of the nonlinear
    port-Hamiltonian modeling of a Rhodes piano", JSV 2017, and its summary.
    The pickup's distance and offset; the hammer's and the beam's model
    values (extras/reference/tine_rhodes.txt).
    https://afalaize.github.io/posts/rhodes/
  - G. Shear and M. Wright, "The electromagnetically sustained Rhodes
    piano", NIME 2011, and G. Shear's MS thesis, UCSB 2011. The tine's
    geometry, one measured overtone, the fundamental's Q per note
    (extras/reference/tine_rhodes.txt).
  - T. Irvine, "Bending frequencies of beams, rods, and pipes", 2012. The
    clamped-free beam's beta_n L (extras/reference/cantilever.txt).
  - H. Suzuki, "Vibration and sound radiation of a piano soundboard", JASA
    80(6), 1986, through K. Ege, "La table d'harmonie du piano", thesis,
    2009, and X. Boutillon and K. Ege, JSV 2013. A grand's and an upright's
    soundboard modes and loss factors (extras/reference/soundboard.txt,
    soundboard_upright.txt).
  - N. H. Fletcher and T. D. Rossing, "The Physics of Musical Instruments",
    chapter 12. The stiff string's partials and B, the strike point,
    soundboard modes and radiation.
  - H. Fletcher, E. D. Blackham and R. Stratton, "Quality of piano tones",
    JASA 34, 1962, and R. W. Young, "Inharmonicity of plain wire piano
    strings", JASA 24, 1952. Measured B per note (cited; not open, not
    transcribed).
  - H. A. Conklin, "Design and tone in the mechanoacoustic piano", JASA
    1996. Scale design, the bass break, hammer masses (cited).
  - G. Weinreich, "Coupled piano strings", JASA 62, 1977. The coupled
    unison (up_string.h): the strings on one bridge whose admittance has a
    resistive and a reactive part, the lock, the two-stage decay and the
    beat past the lock (cited; the physics written out, the paper not
    transcribed).
  - N. Giordano, "Mechanical impedance of a piano soundboard", JASA 103,
    1998. The soundboard's impedance, 1 .. 3 x 10^3 kg/s, behind the
    estimate of the bridge's mobility, bridgeMobility (cited).
  - B. Bank and L. Sujbert, "Generation of longitudinal vibrations in
    piano strings: from physics to sound synthesis", JASA 117(4), 2005.
    The string's longitudinal modes driven by its stretching, their ring of
    a few hundred milliseconds (the attack's clank, up_attack.h; the
    phantom partials not built) (cited).
  - J.-M. Jot and A. Chaigne, "Digital delay networks for designing
    artificial reverberators", AES 90th Convention, 1991. The feedback delay
    network, its loss filter in each line set from the decay time, and the
    unitary feedback matrix in its Householder form (Jot's): the board's
    field above its modes (up_board.h) (cited; the form written out, the
    paper not transcribed).
  - Mutable Instruments, Rings, resonator.cc, through belfry. The ceiling on
    the partials (0.45 of the sample rate); read, not copied.
  - The author's belfry, windstrument and monk_mouth libraries (MIT). The
    channel table and its sequence, the law as a derived-channel writer, the
    pool with its note table and its fade, the two Teensy wrappers and their
    handoff (the pending table under an interrupt mask, the command queue
    with its payload rings), the host rig and its tests follow their shape,
    written again here for a piano.
  - PJRC, the Teensy Audio Library (MIT). The wrappers implement its
    AudioStream (update, allocate, transmit, release) and the examples use
    its objects; nothing of it is copied or shipped here.
